<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <fullName>Prior_Utility_Bill__c</fullName>
    <deleteConstraint>SetNull</deleteConstraint>
    <description>The immediately preceding bill for the same meter, ordered by read date. Written by Apex. Every variance figure on this record is measured against it, so a wrong or stale pointer silently produces wrong variance rather than an error.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>Set automatically to the previous bill for this meter. Blank on the first ever bill for a meter, which is why a first bill correctly shows no variance rather than a 100 percent rise.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Prior Utility Bill</label>
    <referenceTo>Utility_Bill__c</referenceTo>
    <relationshipLabel>Successor Bills</relationshipLabel>
    <relationshipName>Successor_Bills</relationshipName>
    <required>false</required>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Lookup</type>
    <!--
      Apex written; there is no declarative way to find "the preceding bill for this meter".

      A lookup and not a master detail: this object already has its one master detail to
      Meter__c, and a cascade delete down a bill chain would be actively wrong.
      deleteConstraint is SetNull so that deleting a middle bill blanks the pointer rather
      than blocking the delete. That blanking is DETECTABLE, which is what lets the
      afterDelete re-link find and repair the chain.

      The chain is invalidated by a back dated insert as well as by a delete: if January and
      March exist and February is added afterwards, March still points at January. Re-linking
      must therefore run on afterInsert, afterUpdate when Read_Date__c or Meter__c changed,
      AND afterDelete.
    -->
</CustomField>
